course: biology
lesson: 38
title: Spiders, scorpions and other eight-legged creatures
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Spiders are not insects: they are arachnids, with eight legs, a body in only two parts, no feelers, no wings, and usually eight eyes.
explain: Look at a garden spider in the middle of its web on a dewy autumn morning: dry spokes, a sticky spiral, and the spider walking on the dry lines so it never gets stuck in its own trap.
explain: Its silk starts as a liquid and hardens as it is pulled out; some silk is stronger than steel of the same weight, and baby spiders even fly away on it like kites.
explain: The family includes scorpions that glow under an ultraviolet torch, ticks that wait in long grass, and the daddy longlegs, which is not a spider at all.
explain: Spiders are mostly harmless to people, but a few are dangerous, so we watch and never handle wild ones, and we forget the myth about swallowing spiders in our sleep.
fact: Spiders are arachnids: they have eight legs, a body in two parts, no feelers and no wings, and most have eight eyes.
fact: An orb spider spins spokes of dry silk and then a sticky spiral, and it walks mostly on the dry spokes; many eat their old web and spin a new one.
fact: Spider silk is a protein made as a liquid and pulled out through spinnerets; some silk is stronger than steel of the same weight and can stretch a lot.
fact: In autumn male house spiders wander indoors looking for females, and they get stuck in the bath because they cannot climb its smooth sides.
fact: Swallowing eight spiders a year in your sleep is a myth: a big, warm, breathing sleeper is a place spiders keep away from.
fact: Spiders stretch their legs out by pumping blood into them, so when a spider dies the pressure is gone and its legs curl underneath it.
fact: Jumping spiders have two huge front eyes that see sharply and in colour, and they stalk and pounce instead of using webs.
fact: Most American tarantulas flick itchy barbed hairs at enemies, and female tarantulas can live 20 to 30 years.
fact: Almost all spiders have venom for their prey, but only a few, like black widows and the Sydney funnel-web, are dangerous to people, and antivenoms exist; no British spider is deadly.
fact: Baby spiders let out silk threads that catch the breeze and float away; this is called ballooning, and it lets them spread out so they do not all compete for the same food; some travel hundreds of kilometres, even far out to sea.
fact: Scorpions are arachnids whose outer shell glows blue-green under ultraviolet light; mothers carry their babies on their backs.
fact: The harvestman, or daddy longlegs, is an arachnid but not a spider: it has a one-piece round body, two eyes, no silk and no venom at all.
fact: Ticks are eight-legged arachnids that climb grass and grab passing animals to drink their blood; some carry Lyme disease, so a tick should be removed by an adult with a tick tool.
fact: The diving bell spider of European ponds lives underwater in a silk tent filled with air from the surface.
exercise: How many legs does a garden spider have? | Eight | Only six | Twelve | Only four
exercise: Which is true of a spider's body? | Two parts, no feelers, no wings | Three parts, two feelers and wings | One round body part with six legs | Two body parts and two long feelers
exercise: How does a spider avoid getting stuck in its own web? | It walks mostly on the dry spokes | It covers its feet in its own sticky glue | It flies across the web on its wings | It never touches its own web at all
exercise: What makes an orb web sparkle on an autumn morning? | Dew drops caught on the silk | The spider polishing it every night | Silk that glows all by itself | Frost made by the spider itself
exercise: What do many orb spiders do with their old web? | Eat it and use the silk again | Leave it for birds | Roll it into a ball for a bed | Throw it to the wind
exercise: How strong is some spider silk? | Stronger than steel of the same weight | Weaker than paper | As weak as a cotton thread, so it snaps easily | Only strong when wet
exercise: Why do we see big house spiders running across the carpet in September? | Males are wandering to look for females | They are running away from the cold winter outside | They have just hatched | They are hunting for crumbs
exercise: Why is a spider stuck in the bath? | It cannot climb the smooth sides | It climbed up out of the plughole by itself | It is having a bath | It likes the cold
exercise: How does a jumping spider catch its food? | It stalks and pounces, using its big front eyes | It waits in a sticky web | It digs a deep pit | It floats along on silk and grabs flies out of the air
exercise: How does a tarantula defend itself? | It flicks tiny itchy hairs at enemies | It sprays a boiling liquid from its tail | It rolls up like a hedgehog | It glows red to scare enemies
exercise: About how long can a female tarantula live? | 20 to 30 years | Only about 2 weeks | Only about 1 year | About 100 years
exercise: Which statement about spider venom is true? | Almost all spiders have venom, but only a few are dangerous to people | No spider has venom | Every spider can kill a person | Only the big British spiders have venom, and they can all kill people
exercise: How do baby spiders float through the air? | On silk threads that catch the breeze | On tiny wings | On the backs of small birds flying past the nest | By jumping very high
exercise: What does a scorpion do under an ultraviolet torch? | Its shell glows blue-green | It turns invisible | It falls asleep | It changes into a spider
exercise: Which is true of a daddy longlegs, the harvestman? | It is not a spider and has no venom at all | It is the most venomous spider in the world | It spins the biggest webs | It is an insect with six legs
exercise: What does a tick do in long grass? | It waits to grab a passing animal and drink its blood | It spins a sticky web between the grass stems to catch flies | It eats the grass seeds | It hunts butterflies
exercise: Where does the diving bell spider live? | Underwater in a silk tent full of air | High in tree tops | Deep inside dark, damp caves together with the bats | In the desert sand
test: My mate says spiders are insects cos they're creepy-crawlies. Is he right? | No, spiders are arachnids with eight legs and two body parts | Yes, all the creepy-crawlies in the garden are insects of some kind | Yes, but only small spiders | No, spiders are crustaceans
test: lol u swallow 8 spiders a year in ur sleep. right? | Myth: a sleeping, breathing person is the last place a spider wants to go | True, exactly eight | True, but only in the summer when windows are left open at night | True, more if you snore
test: A spider sits in the bath. Your sister says it came up the plughole. What's more likely? | It fell in and cannot climb the smooth sides | It swam up through the water pipes from the drain | It was born in the bath | It came in with the shampoo
test: Popular myth: the daddy longlegs is the most venomous spider but its fangs are too small to bite. What is the truth? | It is not a spider and has no venom glands at all | It is deadly but cannot bite | It is a kind of wasp | It is deadly to dogs and cats, but its bite does no harm to people
test: In a cartoon a spider has six legs and two feelers. How would you correct the drawing? | Give it eight legs and no feelers | Give it ten legs and wings | Give it four legs and a long tail | It is already correct
test: What would happen if an orb spider walked over the sticky spiral all the time? | It could get stuck and waste the glue | It would catch more flies | It would spin faster | Nothing, spiders never stick to anything
test: Why do spiderlings balloon away instead of staying near home? | Spreading out means they don't compete for the same food | Their mother chases them off | They are trying to reach the moon on their silk | They want to find water
test: A sailor finds tiny spiders landing on his ship far out at sea. How did they get there? | By ballooning on silk threads in the air | They swam from the shore | They lived in the sails since the ship was built | They were in the fish he caught
test: A tick is stuck on your dog after a walk. What should you do? | Ask an adult to remove it with a tick tool | Pull it off with your fingers and squash it | Leave it, ticks fall off at once | Burn it off with a match
test: A black widow with a red hourglass on its belly is in a documentary. What is true? | It is one of the few spiders dangerous to people, and antivenoms exist | It is harmless like all spiders | It lives in every British house, hiding in the corners and the bath | It is a kind of beetle
test: In a game, a jumping spider builds a huge web to catch prey. What does a real jumping spider do? | It stalks and pounces with no web to catch prey | It builds the biggest webs of all | It digs traps in sand | It catches small fish in ponds with its front legs
test: Why does a dead spider curl its legs underneath it? | Its legs are stretched by blood pressure, which is gone | It is trying to keep warm | It always sleeps like that, curled up | It is pretending to be a ball
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the anatomical distinction between spiders and insects? | Spiders possess eight legs and two body parts and lack antennae | Spiders possess six legs and three body parts | Spiders possess wings but no legs | There is no distinction between them of any kind, the two being one group
exam: Identify the means by which juvenile spiders disperse through the air. | The release of silk threads borne upon the breeze | The use of rudimentary wings | Attachment to the feathers of migrating birds | Leaping from great heights
exam: Ascertain the principal characteristic of the harvestman which distinguishes it from true spiders. | A single rounded body, two eyes, and no silk or venom | Eight eyes and a highly potent venom | The construction of orb webs | Six legs and a pair of wings
exam: It is given that a tick has attached itself to a domestic dog. Select the appropriate course of action. | Removal by an adult using a tick tool | Removal by crushing it hard between the fingers | Burning with a lit match | Leaving it in place indefinitely
